/* Generated by tools/q/qfluff.py. Lore in own words. */
window.FLUFF={"species":{"Aarakocra":"Birdfolk who nest in tall mountain trees, many of them settlers from the Elemental Plane of Air. They serve as scouts and explorers in a strange world. Feathered and taloned, they feel most at home in the sky and fly with grace and speed.","Aasimar":"Mortals who carry a spark of the Upper Planes in their souls. They can arise among any people and often show a halo, glowing eyes, or metallic freckles. They use that spark to shed light, heal wounds, and strike with heavenly anger.","Aetherborn":"Aetherborn live only a few years or even months, so they treat every moment as precious. They chase pleasure and sensation, each in their own way, and try to wring every bit of joy from their short lives.","Astral Elf":"Elves who left the Feywild long ago to live closer to their gods on the Astral Plane. Starlight shines in their eyes. Nothing ages there, so many are very old, and some grow melancholy while others seem to feel little.","Autognome":"Mechanical beings built by rock gnomes. Some break away from their makers through a malfunction or odd circumstance and set out alone. They look like their creators, speak Gnomish, and run on parts as different as clockwork, stardust, or even a beating heart.","Aven":"Humanoids of Naktamun with the wings and heads of birds. Some have hawk heads and short wings for fast flight, others have ibis heads and broad wings for soaring. They love to fly over their foes and use speed to outwit them.","Boggart":"Small, squat goblinoids of Lorwyn-Shadowmoor with horns and animal snouts. Each looks quite different from the next. They love brewing potions and tend to specialize. In Lorwyn they are born into noisy warrens where rules are only suggestions.","Bugbear":"Hulking, long limbed cousins of goblins and hobgoblins, with coarse hair and pointed teeth. Their roots lie in the Feywild, and they kept a fey knack for lurking just out of sight. Despite their size, they are quiet skulkers, and many have slipped away from Maglubiyet.","Centaur":"Ravnican centaurs love open space and the freedom to roam, even in a crowded city. They run through plazas, parks, and ruins, hooves pounding, until a wall stops them. They have a muscular human upper body on a horse's frame.","Changeling":"Shapeshifters who can take on any face they like, so they live unnoticed in many societies. Their craft comes from the Feywild, where the first changelings appeared. In true form they look faded and plain, and some use new faces to reveal part of their souls.","Deep Gnome":"Also called svirfneblin, these gnomes are natives of the Underdark and carry its magic. They can blend into stone and are hard to find by magic. These gifts let them survive for generations in danger, and they can live up to 500 years.","Dhampir":"Living people with vampiric strength who suffer a dark hunger, usually for blood but sometimes for dreams or life energy. Many come from a vampire's descendants, a bite that changed them only in part, or a grim bargain. Each must decide whether to master the hunger or yield to it.","Dragonborn":"Descendants of eggs laid by chromatic and metallic dragons, possibly blessed by Bahamut and Tiamat, or perhaps made by dragons alone. They look like wingless, scaly, horned dragons on two legs. Their color and features echo the dragon they descend from.","Dragonborn (Chromatic)":"Dragonborn who inherit the raw power of black, blue, green, red, and white dragons. Those colors shine in their scales and in the energy of their breath. They embody volcanoes, arctic winds, and lightning storms, and also the quiet menace of swamp and forest.","Dragonborn (Gem)":"Dragonborn descended from gem dragons, who call themselves heirs of Sardior, the Ruby Dragon. Amethyst, crystal, emerald, sapphire, and topaz hues glow in their scales. Their gifts are the power of mind and will, bright insight, and the joy of discovery, but also the dry ache of despair.","Dragonborn (Metallic)":"Dragonborn who claim the tenacity of brass, bronze, copper, gold, and silver dragons, whose colors glint in their scales. They stand for the fire of hearth and forge, cold mountain air, a spark of inspiration, and acid that scours things clean.","Duergar":"Dwarves changed by centuries in the deepest Underdark, which soaked them in strange magic. Mind flayers and other aberrations later experimented on them and left them with psionic powers. They eventually broke free of those masters and built their own lives below and beyond.","Dwarf":"Dwarves live in old kingdoms and halls carved into mountain roots, among ringing picks and roaring forges. They stay loyal to clan and tradition and hate goblins and orcs. Short, broad, and hardy, they are skilled warriors, miners, and workers of stone and metal.","Dwarf (Kaladesh)":"Kaladesh dwarves judge beauty by quality, so the best device is one that works for years with little upkeep. They despise laziness and shortcuts. A good artisan, they say, works steadily and with care, and they take great pride in that work ethic.","Eladrin":"Elves of the Feywild who can step from place to place in a blink. Each resonates with a season that sets their mood and look, and the season can shift, though some stay in one forever. Like other elves, they live over 750 years.","Elf":"A magical people of otherworldly grace who live in ancient forests or silvery spires lit by faerie light. They love nature, magic, art, music, and poetry. Slender and fine featured, they seem hauntingly beautiful to many other folk.","Elf (Kaladesh)":"Kaladesh elves are tuned to the flow of aether, which they call the Great Conduit. They believe the only way to truly grasp it is to join it, shaping the flow while it shapes them. This sensitivity gives them a deep bond with all life.","Elf (Zendikar)":"Fearless, adaptable people who have fared better than most in Zendikar's chaos. They meet the Eldrazi with resilience and courage. Their treetop villages regrow almost as fast as they fall, and their shamans and druids channel green mana, the magic of life and growth.","Elves of Many Realms":"Corellon Larethian made the first elves as fey beings who changed shape at will. After they were exiled for plotting with Lolth, they fled to the Feywild. There they became Humanoids and lost that gift, and they often wept over the loss.","Faerie":"Playful, mischievous beings with insect bodies, antennae, black eyes, and wings. Every faerie is born from a flower and has innate magic, which many use for pranks. In Lorwyn some serve Queen Oura, while in Shadowmoor others may follow Queen Maralen.","Fairy":"Small winged folk of the Feywild who look like tiny elves with insect wings. Each has one special physical trait. The first fairies spoke Elvish, Goblin, or Sylvan, and meeting human visitors led many to learn Common too.","Firbolg":"Distant kin of giants who once wandered primeval forests, where the forest magic wove itself into their souls. That magic still hums inside them, even far from any forest. It lets them move unseen and speak with plants and animals, and they live up to 500 years.","Flamekin":"People made of fire and stone, with bodies that give off harmless magical flames and a strong tie to nature. They hold self-discovery and self-expression as life's noblest goal, a lifelong road they call the Path of Flame. They live in Lorwyn or Shadowmoor.","Genasi":"Genasi are children of genies and mortals who carry the power of the elemental planes in their blood. Those planes are harsh for visitors, with crushing earth, searing flame, endless sky, and boundless sea. Genies move through the mortal world with ease.","Giff":"Tall, broad shouldered folk with hippo-like features, impossible to miss wherever they go. Their home world has faded into legend, and the gods who made them are forgotten. Those gods' petrified bodies drift on the Astral Sea, unrecognized.","Gith":"The gith rose up against the mind flayers who enslaved them, led by the hero they are named for. After winning freedom, two factions disagreed about what kind of civilization to build. The quarrel became open hatred, and they remain bitter enemies who fight to the death on sight.","Githyanki":"Once free from mind flayer slavery, the githyanki split from the githzerai and fled to the Astral Plane. There they sharpened their psionic power and built the city of Tu'narath. They now spread across the multiverse from outposts called creches. Lanky and strong, they have yellow, green, or brown skin.","Githzerai":"After the schism with the githyanki, githzerai moved to Limbo, a swirling storm of matter and energy. Their strong psionic will carved out a home within the chaos. Slender and speckled in yellow, green, or brown, some now explore other planes and worlds.","Gnome":"Magical folk shaped by gods of invention, illusion, and life underground, including Garl Glittergold. Early gnomes hid in forests and burrows and beat predators with traps and mazelike tunnels. Petite, big eyed, and pointed eared, they make up in cleverness what they lack in size.","Goblin":"Most Ravnicans see goblins as wretched and unimportant, but goblins expect to be recognized someday. They survive among bigger, stronger creatures through tenacity, agility, cunning, and luck. Small and wiry, about three feet tall, they have warty green or red skin.","Goblin (Dankwood)":"Goblins of the Dankwood forest, which may once have held a Netherese wizard's sanctum. They are gentler and more artistic than most goblins. They are curious about everything and share a magical bond with the small creatures of the woods.","Goblin (Ixalan)":"Native to Ixalan, these goblins are curious and eager, and they joined the Brazen Coalition pirates on first contact. Some still live in remote jungle colonies. About three feet tall, they crouch and scramble with prehensile tails, and make fine lookouts and rope hands on pirate ships.","Goliath":"Towering distant descendants of giants, each carrying the favors of the first giants as supernatural boons. Some resemble stone giants, others fire giants. They can briefly grow toward giant size, and they follow their own path, free of the old feuds among giantkind.","Hadozee":"Descended from tiny treetop mammals that evolved gliding flaps, hadozees are now sapient and eager to explore beyond their dangerous homeworld. Natural climbers, they have dexterous feet and skin membranes between arms and legs that stretch taut to glide.","Halfling":"Halflings want a quiet home, a warm fire, a good meal, and good company, far from monsters and war. Some farm in remote villages, while others travel in nomadic bands, drawn by the open road. Even wanderers love peace, food, and hearth.","Harengon":"Rabbitfolk from the Feywild who embody freedom and travel and carry its exuberance into other worlds. They have long feet, colorful fur, sharp senses, and strong legs, and they brim with energy. A little fey luck often lands them just clear of danger.","Hexblood":"People infused with fey magic through eldritch energy or odd witchcraft, often after a deal with a powerful hag. Their strange power shows up as eerie clairvoyance and beguiling magic, and it marks them as people touched by the fey.","Hobgoblin":"Born in the ancient courts of the Feywild alongside goblins and bugbears, many were driven out and marshaled as soldiers by Maglubiyet. They still follow the Feywild rule of reciprocity, where a gift bonds giver and receiver. This helps them build tight communities and vast legions.","Human":"The youngest of the common peoples, humans live short lives and push to achieve much in their years. Perhaps they feel they must prove themselves to the elder races. They build mighty empires through conquest and trade, and they are innovators, achievers, and pioneers.","Human (Amonkhet)":"The most diverse and adaptable of Amonkhet's five humanoid peoples. Humans train in many fighting styles, use any color of mana, and serve all five gods. No one facing a human can predict the fight, which may show cunning or ruthlessness.","Human (Innistrad)":"Most of Innistrad's people are human, and player characters usually should be too. Still, the folk of Kessig, Gavony, Nephalia, and Stensia differ a great deal. Variety among characters comes from their home province instead of their species.","Human (Ixalan)":"Humans live across Ixalan, in Torrezon, now held by the Legion of Dusk, and in the Sun Empire. The Legion's vampires were once human, and the peasants they rule still are. Some who fled the Legion formed the Free Cities and later the Brazen Coalition.","Human (Kaladesh)":"Kaladesh humans are passionate and curious. At their best they chase grand, nearly impossible goals. At their worst they are shallow, impractical dreamers who ignore consequences. Their ambition has shaped the plane's history and still guides its fate.","Human (Keldon)":"The people of Keld are human but unlike any others. Adults stand over six feet tall, with heavy muscle and gray skin from ashy white to blue gray. They shrug off cold and prize self-reliance, strength, and courage above all.","Human (Zendikar)":"The most numerous, diverse, and adaptable folk of Zendikar, and the core of most exploring expeditions. Many hide behind walls in tiny villages and outposts, but many go out to explore and defend the world. As the Eldrazi spread, more are driven from safety.","Kalashtar":"Kalashtar come from the union of humans with renegade quori spirits from the plane of dreams. They look human with symmetrical, slightly angular features, and their eyes glow with strong emotion. They sense their spirit as instinct and inspiration, which grants minor psionic power.","Kender":"Small humanoids who look like pointy eared humans, born when Reorx's wild creation changed some gnomes. They gained near supernatural curiosity and fearlessness. That inquisitiveness drives them to adventure, and many tumble through portals into new worlds.","Kenku":"Raven-like feathered folk with sharp observation and perfect memories. No kenku recalls their origin, and they joke that there are as many tales as kenku. Some call it a curse of flightless mimicry, others a blessed mission to watch and catalog wonders.","Khenra":"Tall, lean folk of Amonkhet with jackal heads and sleek dark fur. They think biting is an unworthy way to fight. Nearly all are born twins, and each pair shares a close emotional bond, so one twin's death in the trials is a terrible shock.","Khoravar":"Descendants of humans and elves who built their own communities in Khorvaire, and who dislike the term half-elf. Their name means children of Khorvaire in Elvish. Many see themselves as a bridge between cultures and become bards, diplomats, mediators, or translators.","Kithkin":"Short folk with stout legs, long arms, and broad, expressive faces. Most are joined by an empathic web that lets them feel nearby kithkin, so they trust each other deeply and see betraying their own as a heinous crime. Some leave the web, often after Phyrexian trauma.","Kobold":"Among the smallest draconic creatures, kobolds show their ancestry in glinting scales and roars. Legends say the first came from the Underdark near early dragons. Some serve or worship dragons, while others help defend against them. Their scales are usually rust colored.","Kor":"Nomadic, reverent people devoted to the land and its sacred sites, living spare lives on the move. With ropes and hooks they climb cliffs and cross chasms so deftly they seem to fly. Tied to white mana, they value harmony, order, and strong tradition.","Leonin":"Lion-like nomads who guard Oreskos, a golden plain that even gods rarely enter. Prides keep to themselves, distrusting strangers. Tall and tawny furred, some with manes from gold to black, a few wonder what lies past the border mountains and go out to test themselves.","Lizardfolk":"Scaled people some sages call distant cousins of dragonborn and kobolds, though they are their own folk and have lived on the Material Plane since its creation. The gods gave them tough bodies and a link to nature, so they can survive on wits alone. Myths say they guard natural wonders.","Locathah":"Proud, resilient fish-folk who have endured war, slavery, and mistreatment at the hands of other aquatic creatures. They live in tribal communities beneath the water along seacoasts. They hunt both above and below the waves.","Lorwyn Changeling":"Charismatic shapeshifters who crudely mimic creatures and plants, but only on the surface. They always keep blue green skin, tentacle-like fur tufts, and bulbous yellow eyes, so a changeling tree or elk is easy to spot. Many Lorwyn folk find the plain disguises charming.","Loxodon":"Elephant headed humanoids over seven feet tall, with trunks, tusks, and thick leathery skin. They are calm presences in Ravnica's busy streets, humming or sitting very still. If provoked, they bellow and trumpet with rage. They are wise, loyal, and unshakable guild members.","Lupin":"Bristling, snarling folk who cast imposing shadows in the Mists. They often arise when a werewolf's curse only partly takes hold, leaving them stuck in a wolflike hybrid form. This gives them bestial might, sharp instincts, and a dread-inducing howl.","Merfolk":"Zendikar's merfolk are curious, thoughtful, analytical scholars and explorers. They once organized around three deities, then learned their faith rested on a corrupted memory of the Eldrazi titans. Facing the Eldrazi, they set aside old quarrels and united.","Merfolk (Ixalan)":"The River Heralds are amphibious humanoids at home in Ixalan's oceans, rivers, and rain forests. They stand seven to eight feet tall, with skin from burgundy and violet to green and orange. Fins run along their arms, backs, and calves, and they breathe air or water.","Minotaur":"Strong, dedicated, and brave, Ravnican minotaurs are at home on the battlefield. They fight with burning fury yet keen tactics, making them fine commanders and shock troops. Barrel chested with bull heads, they often decorate or sheathe their horns in metal.","Minotaur (Amonkhet)":"Powerfully built, barrel chested humanoids with ram heads and horns curled around their ears. They are strong, stubborn, boastful, and recklessly brave, and they relish a fight against long odds. Rowdy and blunt to the point of rudeness, they say what they want and dare others to stop them.","Monstrous Adventurers":"Some campaigns let normally sinister humanoids join the party as heroes. This option is for DMs who want to widen their selection of species. Playing a creature usually cast as a villain offers comedy, tragedy, or antihero stories, so talk it over first.","Naga":"Giant snake bodies with a humanoid torso, arms, and shoulders, adorned with armor and jewelry. They hold their upper bodies off the ground and can lower them to move faster. Naga follow the Sweetest Harmony, a perfect balance between body and mind.","Orc":"Gruumsh made orcs and gave them gifts for roaming plains, caverns, and seas and facing monsters there. They keep endurance, determination, and darkvision even when they follow other gods. Tall, broad, and gray skinned with tusklike canines, they hear tales of their ancestors' long journeys.","Orc (Ixalan)":"Orcs came from the mountains where Torrezon was born and have fought the Legion of Dusk since it began. The Church of Torrezon declared them anathema, and only a few hundred remain. Many serve on Brazen Coalition ships, doing work that needs plenty of muscle.","Owlin":"Distant kin of giant owls from the Feywild, in sizes from small and fluffy to wide winged and majestic. They have arms and legs like other Humanoids, plus wings, and their feathers make no sound. Some are nocturnal, others simply night owls who rise late.","Plasmoid":"Shapeless beings who take on a similar shape to whoever is nearby, yet are never mistaken for anything else. They eat by osmosis like an amoeba and breathe through pores. Usually translucent gray, they can change color by absorbing dyes, and their flexible limbs handle tools and weapons.","Reborn":"Reborn have died and yet live on, and nobody can say why. Some wear death's marks, such as ashen skin or bloodless veins, while others are built by magic or science from many parts. All of them chase answers and a life of their own.","Rimekin":"Rimekin are flamekin who answered the Phyrexian invasion with cold reasoning instead of rash reaction. Their innate flames turned icy blue and give off chill instead of heat, and armor and weapons in their hands gather spiky, harmless frost. They live in both Lorwyn and Shadowmoor.","Satyr":"Satyrs are cheerful, social folk who love a good revel and treat life as a gift to enjoy. They look humanlike, but goat horns, pointed ears, hooves, and furred legs set them apart. They tease the solemn builders and philosophers of Theros and want to taste everything the world offers.","Shifter":"Shifters descend from people touched by lycanthropy. They cannot take a full beast form, but they can briefly sharpen their animal traits, which they call shifting. They are lithe and human in size, with big eyes, pointed ears, long canines, and fur-like hair, yet always clearly Humanoid.","Simic Hybrid":"Simic hybrids are humans, elves, or vedalken reshaped by Simic Combine magic with animal traits, mostly from sea, reptile, or amphibian creatures. Claws, tentacles, fins, and toothy maws are common. The Combine made them as adapted soldiers for its Guardian Project and treats any who leave as deserters.","Siren":"Sirens are birdlike humanoids of the Stormwreck Sea, living on rocky coasts, remote islands, and floating kelp. Their moods swing fast from warm to cruel and back. They love ships and toy with them, though a growing number now join crews to feed their curiosity, one even as captain.","Tabaxi":"The Cat Lord, a divine being of the Upper Planes, made tabaxi to blend humanoids with cats. Some tabaxi live like true cats, curious and playful, while others live like any other folk. Their fur might show big cat stripes, a house cat's coat, or a pattern all their own.","Thri-kreen":"Thri-kreen are insect folk with four arms and a chitin shell that shifts color to match their surroundings. They never sleep, but rest in still periods while staying fully alert. They speak through clacking mandibles and waving antennae, which others cannot read, so they use telepathy with outsiders.","Tiefling":"Tieflings are born in the Lower Planes or descend from Fiends, and the link is in their blood. This legacy brings power but does not set their morals, and each tiefling decides whether to embrace or regret it. Three legacies exist: abyssal, chthonic, and infernal, each with its own looks.","Tortle":"Tortles are turtle folk who carry their homes on their backs and roam coasts, rivers, and seas on many worlds. They tell no single tale of their origin, but all feel a mystic bond with nature. Their shell gives them security anywhere, and many decorate it with care.","Triton":"Tritons are amphibious folk of the Siren Sea, living among coral forests and hidden undersea mountains. Scaled, crested, and finned, they swim as fast as they run and craft gear from coral, shell, and bone. Most stay below, but curious ones climb out to explore the dry world.","Uncommon Races":"Dragonborn, gnomes, half-elves, and half-orcs are less common than dwarves, elves, halflings, and humans. Big cities barely notice them, but small villages react more. Dragonborn draw wary caution, gnomes win people over with humor, half-elves attract gossip, and half-orcs are often assumed to be short-tempered.","Vampire":"Zendikar's vampires were bred into servitude and now live on the energy in blood, strongest in fear and pain. They are not undead but carry a disease that chills their gray or purple skin. A civil war splits them between free traditionalists and those bound again to the Eldrazi.","Vampire (Ixalan)":"The Legion of Dusk is ruled by vampires who are faster, stronger, and gifted with supernatural power. Some see the Rite of Redemption as sacrifice for a glorious age and carry an imperial pride. Others use vampirism to gain an edge, and they are cruel and ambitious conquerors seeking the Immortal Sun.","Vedalken":"Vedalken believe nothing is perfect and enjoy that, since every flaw invites improvement. They are tall, slender, hairless, and blue skinned, with broad noses and no outer ears, and they talk about ideas but keep their lives private. Others find them cold, though they feel deeply and simply hide it well.","Verdan":"The verdan are goblins and hobgoblins changed when they passed through the shadow of That-Which-Endures, a mysterious entity of chaos. They now have jade skin, black blood, pointed ears, and limited telepathy, but lost their history. They fled to the surface and are the newest people in Faerûn.","Warforged":"Warforged are sentient constructs built as weapons for the Last War, yet they feel pain and emotion. Cords soaked in alchemical fluid act as muscle around a frame of steel, darkwood, or stone, with armored plates over it. Those who grow into individuals often seek an artificer to reshape their bodies.","Yuan-Ti":"Yuan-ti were once humans who remade themselves into serpent folk through ancient rites. Most of those rites twisted them into monsters, but some produced a people that mixes human and snake traits. They resist magic and poison and show it through forked tongues, snake eyes, or similar features."},"bg":{"Aberrant Heir":"Your aberrant dragonmark has made life hard. You may have hidden it well, or met fear and hostility from dragonmarked houses. You might have joined House Tarkanan's criminals, or you might walk your own road, trusting your wits and your mark to carry you.","Acolyte":"You spent your life serving a temple to a god or pantheon, performing rites and offerings that bring worshipers close to the divine. This is not the same as being a cleric. You may have been a child helper, a sudden high priest, or the head of a small cult.","Agent of the Ninth Quill":"Your sharp mind and thieving skill led you to the Ninth Quill, a network of thieves, saboteurs, and spies who take magical lore and relics from others. Stealing items and tomes and sabotaging mages taught you cunning and a few magical tricks for spy work.","Anthropologist":"You are fascinated by cultures, from ancient lost lands to modern cities. You studied customs, laws, rituals, beliefs, languages, and art to learn how societies shape their own fates. Books and scrolls taught you part of it, and visits to distant settlements taught the rest.","Archaeologist":"You spent your life studying fallen cultures by visiting ruins, reading their records, and examining what they left behind. You may have trained at Morgrave University and worked in remote digs. Some archaeologists loot for fame or coin, but most feel called to learn, mixing scholarship with treasure hunting grit.","Artisan":"As soon as you could carry a bucket, you cleaned floors in an artisan's workshop for a few coppers a day. Later you apprenticed, learned to make simple goods, and learned to charm picky customers. Your trade also gave you a sharp eye for detail.","Astral Drifter":"You have wandered the Astral Sea longer than you can recall. There you stopped aging, hunger, and thirst, and you drifted between its regions out of wanderlust. You have camped on the husks of dead gods, dodged psychic winds, and avoided the plane's deadliest inhabitants.","Athlete":"You push yourself to perfect body and technique, and you feel alive in competition with a cheering crowd. Stories of your feats open doors and loosen tongues. In Theros, contests and those who pursue them earn near reverent respect, and athletes of every origin often meet each other.","Augen Trust (Spy)":"The Augen Trust is a secret network of imperial spies in the Dwendalian Empire, led by the gnome Oliver Schreiber, who alone knows every member. Betraying it is high treason, and defectors are hunted. If you are an adventurer, you either hid your identity or remain on duty.","Azorius Functionary":"You proudly serve the Azorius Senate, the guild that writes and enforces the laws of Ravnica. With brutes at the gate and chaos looming, you believe order is all that prevents disaster. You help keep the city running safely and smoothly.","Baldur's Gate Acolyte":"Baldur's Gate holds believers of nearly every faith, with newcomers constantly arriving from distant lands. Most faiths are tolerated, though evil ones are pushed into the Twin Songs district. An acolyte from here might seek greater things, not for personal glory but for the sake of their faith.","Baldur's Gate Charlatan":"In Baldur's Gate everyone tries to get ahead of someone else. One group's swindler is another's revolutionary, or perhaps you simply look out for yourself. Either way, you have a plan to hit it big, and all you need is nerve and a bit of time.","Baldur's Gate Criminal":"Any career criminal in Baldur's Gate knows the Guild. Some keep quiet, slipping smuggled goods in with honest cargo, and others join crews or the Guild for protection. A few were thrown out for incompetence or for crossing someone powerful, and they now scrape by on scraps.","Baldur's Gate Entertainer":"Baldur's Gate offers a colorful range of performers, from the Oasis Theater's singers and acrobats to the Wide's mimes and shadow puppeteers. Good acts always find audiences, and the steady stream of travelers brings both fresh spectators and fresh spectacles through the city.","Baldur's Gate Folk Hero":"Baldur's Gate badly needs heroes, and now and then one rises from its own people. Locals love and remember such champions, but public opinion can turn on them nearly as fast as it first praised them.","Baldur's Gate Guild Artisan":"Baldur's Gate has many guilds and professional groups, covering every trade you can name, from gravediggers to moneylenders. As an artisan here, you belong to one of these organized crafts.","Baldur's Gate Hermit":"A hermit in a busy city may seem odd, but deep solitude can exist in the middle of a crowd. A small number of people in Baldur's Gate manage to find isolation amid all its noise and bustle.","Baldur's Gate Noble":"The patriars of Baldur's Gate live in the Upper City, hosting galas and keeping fashion, walled off from the poorer districts. They know the courts of far lands well but little of the poor at their door. Their feature replaces Position and Privilege, though a merchant noble may keep the standard one.","Baldur's Gate Outlander":"People come to Baldur's Gate for profit, excitement, and cosmopolitan chances, but few grasp the tangled social mess waiting there. You arrive as an outsider, and no matter how long you stay, you will likely remain one, if you ever leave at all.","Baldur's Gate Sage":"Baldur's Gate has a modest scholarly community around the High Hall libraries and temples of learning. Lecturers, researchers, and historians trade ideas with visiting scholars from Candlekeep. Arcane study also thrives, though wizards work alone in their own homes rather than in a shared community.","Baldur's Gate Sailor":"Sailors founded Baldur's Gate, and its harbor remains its heart. Several patriars descend from old captains, the Lower City's trade rests on the port, and river traffic sets the Outer City's pace. Sailors are as common here as the cobblestones, so this background is widespread.","Baldur's Gate Soldier":"Many kinds of soldiers walk the streets of Baldur's Gate, including mercenaries, private guards, Watch soldiers, and members of the Flaming Fist. You are one of them, and you earn your keep with arms and discipline.","Baldur's Gate Urchin":"Orphans and runaways gather in the Outer City, chasing passersby in ragged crowds to beg for scraps. In the Lower City, urchins are often recruited into the Guild's lowest ranks or forced into dangerous work by ruthless masters.","Bejeweled Conclave Spy":"In public you pose as a courtier, mediator, or entertainer, but your real talent is gathering secrets. The enchanters of the Bejeweled Conclave trained you to use charm, wit, and a little magic to infiltrate groups, uncover information, and broker deals.","Boros Legionnaire":"You serve the Boros Legion, devoted to angels and to bringing justice and peace to Ravnica's streets. You might be a true believer moved by the angels and the downtrodden. You might instead be a cynic who followed a parent, fell for a recruiter's promises, or wanted an active life.","Carouser":"You grew up in the heart of a big city like Baldur's Gate, spending countless evenings in taverns, theaters, parlors, and gambling dens. You read people well and learn their secrets over cards or at fine parties. You have been thrown out of a few places, but only by dull hosts.","Celebrity Adventurer's Scion":"Your family name inspires fear and admiration, though you earned none of it. Songs celebrate your famous parent, who was seldom home and always talking of dragons and demons. The family fortune went to potions and ale, and everyone expects you to fight like your forebear, so proving yourself is hard.","Charlatan":"You have always had a way with people. You sense what they want, draw out their desires in minutes, and promise to deliver it. Your pink tonics, silvery ointments, and bridges for sale sound too good to be true, yet you make them sound real.","Chondathan Freebooter":"Chondath expects youths to serve four years in the military, but you resented that control. You gave up your nation and your name, then joined the first ship that would take you. You have roamed the Vilhon Reach ever since, with deep local ties and broad experience despite staying near land.","City Watch":"You served the community you grew up in as its first defense against crime, policing its people rather than facing outside enemies. You may have walked with Waterdeep's City Watch, Silverymoon's Silverwatch or Spellguard, or Neverwinter's Wintershield. Many towns have constables, sheriffs, and bailiffs who do this same work.","Clan Crafter":"The Stout Folk are famed for their craftsmanship, and you trained in that old tradition under a dwarf master, enduring long hours and sour treatment to earn your skill. You are probably a dwarf, but shield dwarf clans accept promising apprentices of any race. Non-dwarves swear never to teach the craft onward.","Cloistered Scholar":"As a child you were curious while your playmates were rowdy. You went to one of Faerûn's great centers of learning, such as Candlekeep, the Vault of the Sages, or Silverymoon's Map House, and learned that knowledge outweighs gold. Now you leave to seek new lore and share your expertise.","Cobalt Scholar (Sage)":"The Cobalt Soul is an order of monastic scholars who preserve knowledge and history across Exandria, based mainly in Zadash and Rexxentrum. You likely studied there as a pupil and adventure to broaden your mind. With your DM's leave, you could instead be an expositor or an archivist.","Cosmic Dawn Experiment":"The transmutation practices of the Bringers of the Cosmic Dawn have warped your body. You might have been caught in one of their experiments, or you might have joined and sought to improve yourself through magic. Either way, you now use your odd anatomy to your advantage.","Courtier":"Once you held a position of some importance in a noble court or a bureaucracy, earned by talent rather than birth. You may have served in Silverymoon, Waterdeep, Baldur's Gate, Neverwinter, or Daggerford. Your old ties can help you and your companions, and the skills you honed there still serve you well.","Covenant of the Grave Recruit":"Death has always fascinated you. You studied the morbid with members of the Covenant of the Grave, treating the macabre with scientific precision. Through this work you gained knowledge of medicine, necromancy, and history.","Criminal":"You are an experienced lawbreaker with contacts across the criminal underworld. You know the murder, theft, and violence under civilization's surface better than most, and you have survived by ignoring the rules of society.","Crucible Storm Chaser":"Your daredevil streak and hunger for thrills brought you to the Crucible Keepers, who study natural and magical disasters. You have handled molten glass and charged into the eye of a hurricane. You feel most alive when catastrophe is only steps away.","Dead Magic Dweller":"Anauroch's dead magic zones are hostile to casters and magical monsters, and that is why you settled there. Maybe Red Wizards hunted you, or you angered a djinni in Calimshan. After months or years, you came out stronger and wiser, knowing desert medicine and wasteland survival.","Dimir Operative":"You are a spy of House Dimir, dealing in secrets and lies, infiltrating guilds and stealing from journals and minds alike. Your missions sometimes only hide another strike or spread fear. You keep a false identity as a member of another guild, which supplies your gear and contacts.","Discredited Neural Tuner":"You once worked at Mordenheim Kinetics doing questionable work in its darkest depths, until something angered the powers above and you were punished. Company security dragged you to a waste chamber for failed experiments, but you escaped. Now you live as a disgraced outcast, hunting the truth about your old employer.","Dragon Cultist":"You are an initiate of the Cult of the Dragon, found or brought into a local cell. You show its values of duplicity, secrecy, and determination. In return for your oath, the cult gave you fellow worshipers and resources for studying arcana and the occult.","Emerald Enclave Caretaker":"You serve the Emerald Enclave by caring for those who protect the world. Alone or with others, you learned to track game, forage herbs, and predict weather. You use these skills to keep civilization and wilderness in balance and to destroy unnatural creatures.","Entertainer":"You thrive before an audience and know how to enchant, amuse, and inspire. Your verse can stir grief or joy, your music lifts spirits or mourns, your dance dazzles, and your humor stings. Whatever your methods, your art is your life.","Fabricated Mind":"You are the product of monstrous experimental neurotechnology, a wholly new mind rather than a copy. You woke sentient in the halls of Mordenheim Kinetics, with no idea who made you. Your synthetic nature pushes you to seek an identity, allies of similar origin, or revenge on your makers.","Faceless":"You believe your class, people, family, or sins keep you from ever being a hero, yet you press on. You left your old face behind and took on a new persona, dramatic or subtle, literal or not. This background focuses on the hero beneath the mask.","Faction Agent":"Many Faerûn factions ignore borders and act wherever needed, using listeners, smugglers, sellswords, and haven keepers. You served one as its hands, head, and heart, openly or in secret, before adventuring. You may keep your membership, and adventuring may even raise your standing.","Failed Merchant":"You came from a line of merchants or tried business yourself, but it all ended badly through bad luck, outside forces, or weak skill. You lost everything but gained experience and contacts. Your sense of commerce drew you toward Acquisitions Incorporated, where a franchise may await.","Familiar Trainer":"A magical familiar bonded with you in youth and stayed beside you through adolescence. You cannot imagine life without your furry, feathery, scaly, or slimy friend. You learned to work as a team, and when adventure gets hard, your familiar reminds you of small joys.","Far Traveler":"Most people on the Sword Coast never travel more than a few miles from where they were born, but you come from a remote land few have heard of. You came for your own reasons. Local ways may seem strange, and you may be a figure of curiosity wherever you go.","Farmer":"You grew up close to the land. Years of tending animals and crops gave you patience and good health. You appreciate nature's generosity and respect its fury, and you carry that steady outlook into every trial you face.","Feylost":"You vanished from your home plane as a child and grew up in the Feywild. Fey may have taken you kindly, you may have wandered in by chance, or evil Fey may have stolen you. You returned changed, haunted by the Feywild nearby, and you both fear and long for it.","Fisher":"You spent your life on fishing boats or searching the shallows for the sea's bounty. You may come from a family of fisher folk, or perhaps the work was a way out of a bad situation. Either way, you grew to love the sea, the craft, and the endless horizon.","Flaming Fist Mercenary":"The Flaming Fist is Baldur's Gate's main law enforcement, a mercenary guild led by the city's grand duke. You served in it and learned to head off trouble with a hard stare and to take deadly blows. Its members are among the toughest warriors on the Sword Coast, and you uphold that name.","Folk Hero":"You come from humble roots, yet you are destined for more. Your home village already sees you as its champion. Your fate calls you to face the tyrants and monsters that menace ordinary people everywhere.","Gambler":"You believe a little gold can turn into a lot, or into none. Good or bad, you cannot predict Lady Luck, who sometimes lifts you and sometimes drops you. Still, there is always someone willing to take a bet.","Gate Warden":"You lived for a long time near a portal or in a place steeped in planar energy, like a gatetown in the Outlands. Sights that terrify or dazzle other people feel ordinary to you. You deal with Celestials and Fiends as easily as with any shopkeeper.","Genie Touched":"Genie magic still lingers in your homeland, and one day your life crossed paths with a genie. It may have been a djinni in a lamp, a marid at an oasis, a dao, or an efreeti. The meeting left you sharp-eyed, smooth-tongued, and a little magical.","Giant Foundling":"You are not a Giant, yet giants raised you, or you grew up in a land full of huge creatures. Some magic in your surroundings made you unusually large. You learned to channel giant strength, and you see the world from the viewpoint of someone used to towering neighbors.","Golgari Agent":"You belong to the Golgari swarm, one small piece of a vast living system of decay and renewal. You grew up in the dark corners of the city where life's messy side shows. You feel no fear of death or the dead, only a fierce respect for the cycle.","Grinner":"You are a Grinner of the Golden Grin, a secret network that began in Tal'Dorei and now operates from hidden bases across the Clovis Concord. Your group works to reach the top of the Dwendalian Empire. You aim to bring freedom and hope to the oppressed, and spread coded folk songs to warn allies.","Gruul Anarch":"You come from the wild fringes of Ravnica, the ruined districts, rubble fields, and overgrown parks left outside the great city. Your people cling to the Old Ways and live close to nature and primal fury. The rest of the world, busy with progress, pays you little attention.","Guard":"You spent long hours on watch in a tower, and your feet still remember it. You were trained to look two ways at once. One eye scanned beyond the wall for raiders from the forest, and the other searched inside for thieves and troublemakers.","Guide":"You grew up in the open wilderness, and home was wherever you laid your bedroll. You explored strange monsters, clean forests and streams, and old ruins built by giants. Along the way you led friendly nature priests, who taught you the basics of wild magic.","Guild Artisan":"You belong to an artisan guild and work closely with other crafters in your trade. Your skill and wealth put you in the merchant class, free from feudal duties. You trained as an apprentice under a master, with your guild's backing, until you earned the rank of master yourself.","Harper":"You accepted an invitation to join the Harpers and swore to follow their code and serve the common good. You know when to work as a team and when to act alone. Veteran Harpers taught you secret melodies, watchwords, and sleight of hand to watch and thwart evil.","Haunted One":"Your past haunts you, whether it came from one terrible event or a long run of pain. You have tried burying it and running from it, and nothing worked. No sword can cut it down and no spell can banish it, yet you keep going.","Hermit":"You spent a formative stretch of your life apart from society, either in a sheltered community like a monastery or completely alone. Away from the noise of the world, you found stillness and solitude. You may also have found some of the answers you were seeking.","Horizon Weaver Initiate":"As a restless teenager you left home as soon as you could and went traveling. On the road you met the Horizon Weavers, adventurous conjurers. They taught you some of their teleportation magic to help you explore farther.","House Agent":"You are tied to a dragonmarked house but have not yet shown a dragonmark. You may be family by blood or simply an employee. Your work has been running the house's business as the eyes, hands, and feet of its leaders, and you have friends, mentors, and rivals inside it and beyond.","House Cannith Heir":"You are a scion of House Cannith, which builds the marvels of the modern age. The house expects you to add to its success through invention, study, business, or diplomacy. A deep split inside the house troubles you, as three barons compete for leadership and you probably favor one.","House Deneith Heir":"As an heir of House Deneith you trained for battle, though you neither chase it nor fear it. You value duty, honor, and the laws that serve when those fail. You may have guarded a minor dignitary, fought as a mercenary, or hunted fugitives as a marshal. You expect your due place in the house.","House Ghallanda Heir":"House Ghallanda raised you among comfort, good talk, fine drink, and tasty meals. You might be a witty, charming host who loves a warm hearth, or a schemer who uses trust to bend others. Adventure may let you learn foreign cuisines, grow your contacts, or save up for your own inn.","House Jorasco Heir":"House Jorasco teaches that sickness and wounds haunt the living and steal health and long life. You learned to fight them with magic and medicine, and to make sure you are paid well. You might be a gentle healer who avoids battle, or a cold specialist who knows exactly how to disable or kill.","House Kundarak Heir":"You are proud of your Kundarak family and its work guarding the valuables of Khorvaire. The house leads among the clans of the Mror Holds and is known for professionalism, honor, and order. You may uphold that name, put your own honor first, or rebel against what the house demands.","House Lyrandar Heir":"As a Lyrandar heir, you count the wind as an ally and the sea and sky as your realm. Your house is rising thanks to its airship mastery. You may have worked as a sailor, navigator, shipwright, Raincaller, or airship crew, and the house may send you after pirates or ruins.","House Medani Heir":"Your life as a Medani heir centers on intrigue, but your job is stopping it. You see the world as a web of schemes and counterplots, and you use instinct to spot threats to your clients early. Your house prefers that no blade is ever drawn, rather than blocking one afterward.","House Orien Heir":"House Orien once connected Khorvaire with trade roads and lightning rails. The Last War broke those links and ended Galifar. Couriers still carry goods, and the house wants routes across the Mournland restored. Orien adventurers look into missing couriers, stolen caravan cargo, and trouble on the lightning rail.","House Phiarlan Heir":"You grew up with wealth, fame, and beauty in House Phiarlan, yet you value knowledge and the power it gives most of all. You may be a performer or artist who listens for secrets while entertaining crowds. You may instead link up with the house's spy branch, the Serpentine Table.","House Sivis Heir":"Your family has spent almost three thousand years keeping order, because communication holds civilization together. Your ancestors settled royal disputes and helped dragonmarked houses find their place. While other houses chase fame, Sivis plays a quiet, long game of influence behind the scenes and shapes the age.","House Tharashk Heir":"Other heirs live in luxury, but House Tharashk taught you to rely on yourself. The young house has little wealth and plenty of drive, and it has a destiny you share. In battle or conversation you act as a hunter who studies foes, finds weaknesses, and wins by any means.","House Thuranni Heir":"House Thuranni is young and built on espionage, so its leaders expect you to advance its interests whenever you can. Everything you did as the house found its footing was watched closely. Even the quietest artist or artisan in the house follows politics and passes along information when asked.","House Vadalis Heir":"You grew up valuing both family and nature. You know how the Five Nations live but stay out of their games of status and ambition. You understand life's power and its mysteries, and your feel for nature lets you see through city folk's deceit without ever showing it.","Ice Fisher":"You come from a proud line of ice fishers in Ten-Towns, Icewind Dale. Pulling knucklehead trout from frozen lakes is not glamorous, but it pays honestly. You learned to sense the faintest tug on a line, and the hard work toughened your body and mind for adventuring.","Inheritor":"You are the heir to something precious, though not money. A single object was entrusted to you, perhaps by family, a friend, or a mentor. Learning of it changed your life and may have launched your adventures. It also brings danger from those who want to take it from you.","Initiate":"You are an initiate working through the trials of the five gods, hoping for a glorious death in the final Trial of Zeal. Your talents and teachers pointed you toward close combat, ranged combat, or spellcasting. A truly worthy initiate stays well rounded, so multiclassing or feats can fill the gaps.","Inquisitive":"Boundless curiosity drives you, and you sharpened your skills of investigation and deduction to solve mysteries, find missing people, recover stolen goods, and expose corruption and conspiracies. You may have worked freelance, for an inquisitive agency such as one licensed by House Medani or Tharashk, for a newspaper, or for the police.","Inquisitor":"Inquisitors were once cathar detectives who looked into both ordinary and supernatural crimes, often in remote parishes, and who unmasked hidden werewolves. While Avacyn was trapped in the Helvault, they became brutal, executing suspects and holding unsanctioned trials. After her madness, that cruelty became normal, and the true investigators are now few.","Investigator":"You pursue the truth without rest. You may have seen something strange or awful and need to understand it, or you may simply care about justice and honesty. Whether the case is a local crime or an eldritch conspiracy, you work to expose what others want hidden.","Izzet Engineer":"You bring an inventive mind, a love of magical technology, and endless energy to the research of the Izzet League. You likely start as an attendant, but you might rise to mage, alchemist, lab supervisor, or flamethrower-carrying scorchbringer. You embody the Izzet spirit of brilliant, obsessive, chaotic inspiration.","Knight of Solamnia":"You trained as a Knight of Solamnia, a valorous warrior. Strict rules shape everything you do, and you work to follow them while protecting the weak and opposing evil. Honor matters to you as much as your own life.","Knight of the Gauntlet":"You did not want to spend your days reading scripture in a quiet temple, so you joined the Order of the Gauntlet as a holy warrior. You scorn evil, stand by your fellow knights, and care for survivors of war. You swore to rest only when justice outshines chaos across Faerun.","Knight of the Order":"You belong to a knightly order sworn to a vital cause. Faerun has many such orders, and most welcome all kinds of folk who will fight and die for the goal, not just armored nobles. Some serve a god, some a government, and others follow a philosophy.","Lords' Alliance Vassal":"You swore loyalty to a member city of the Lords' Alliance and pledged to increase safety and prosperity along the Sword Coast. You bring honor to your lord's house, whether by guarding trade roads for a Waterdeep merchant-lord or hunting monsters near Daggerford. You are as skilled with a sword as with a quill.","Lorehold Student":"You spent your youth preparing for Lorehold College and read every book on its list for applicants. History is your great passion. You might dream of using magic to speak with famous figures of the past or your own ancestors, or to recreate the mighty magic of a distant era.","Lorwyn Expert":"You come from Lorwyn, the land of endless sunshine, or you have studied it closely. You see the creature Eirdu as the living sun and love whimsical adventures. You cherish loved ones, having watched some wander into Shadowmoor and fade. You stay brave and eager for new experiences.","Luxonborn (Acolyte)":"Every acolyte serves a god, but you serve the Luxon, whom you believe stands above godhood and made the cosmos. You study potential, which the Luxon embodies. Your Shelter of the Faithful feature covers all who revere the Luxon and all who practice dunamancy, the art it gave the world.","Mage of High Sorcery":"The Mages of High Sorcery noticed your magical talent, and you trained with this group that studies magic and prevents its misuse. You may yet face the dangerous test for full membership. Your passion likely leans you toward the White, Red, or Black Robes. They accept sorcerers and warlocks too.","Marine":"You trained to fight on sandy beaches and rocky shores. You led midnight raids from fast ships whose names frighten your enemies. The sea is your second home, rain is your shelter, and crashing waves are your battle cry.","Mercenary Veteran":"You fought battles for coin and know the risk of life and limb for a share of treasure. Now you hope for greater rewards as an adventurer, or for freedom to pick your own work. You have gripping battlefield tales, maybe from the Zhentarim, Mintarn's soldiers, or a small band.","Merchant":"You apprenticed with a trader, caravan master, or shopkeeper and learned how commerce works. You traveled widely, buying and selling raw materials for crafters or their finished goods. You may have moved cargo by ship, wagon, or caravan, or kept a shop of your own.","Mist Wanderer":"You once had a home, until the Mists rose and pulled you into a Domain of Dread. Since then you have drifted from domain to domain, braving the shifting Mists in search of a way back. The cursed realms changed you, but fellow wanderers offer comfort.","Moonwell Pilgrim":"You grew up in the Moonshae Isles, honoring the blessed land, its gods, and the shrines called moonwells. You set out on a pilgrimage to visit and commune with every moonwell. On the way you gathered folk songs, painted lovely vistas, and picked up a bit of primal magic.","Mulhorandi Tomb Raider":"You grew up under living god-kings, hearing tales of ancient empires and buried cities full of forgotten treasure. You decided to explore the crypts, tombs, and pyramids of your homeland yourself. Your goal is to reclaim your people's relics from those who are cunning and brave enough to seek them.","Mutated Commuter":"You rode a gloomy train to work each day until it crashed. You crawled out of the wreck unhurt but changed, your body mutated by the supernatural violence. You woke near Mordenheim Kinetics, surrounded by sea and misty forest, and entered its campus seeking help, though hope there is thin.","Myriad Operative (Criminal)":"You are no common cutpurse. You belong to the Myriad, the greatest crime syndicate in Exandria, with bases in every major city outside Eastern Wynandir. You have criminal contacts and allies, but also rivals and bosses who would sacrifice you. Anywhere the Myriad operates, you can find shelter from the law for a price.","Mythalkeeper":"Mythals are great magical powers that can reshape the Weave and even reality. Most are ancient, and many are damaged or dormant. You come from the Dalelands, where you likely met your first mythal in Myth Drannor. You travel Faerun seeking other ruined places of power, to learn their history or repair them.","Noble":"You know wealth, power, and privilege. Your family holds a noble title, land, and political sway. You might be a pampered aristocrat, a newly raised merchant, a disinherited scoundrel, or a caring landowner who feels responsible for your people. Work with your DM to shape your title and family.","Orzhov Representative":"The Orzhov Syndicate promises immense wealth and moves money from Ravnican society up to the top of its hierarchy. As a low-ranking functionary, you hope to keep as much as you can from the money passing through your hands. That is how you plan to climb until you prove your value.","Outlander":"You grew up in the wilds, far from towns and technology. You saw herds bigger than forests, endured weather no city dweller could imagine, and loved being the only thinking creature for miles. The wild runs in your blood, whether you were a nomad, explorer, hermit, hunter, or raider.","Phantasmic Circus Trouper":"You found a home with the Phantasmic Circus, as a performer or a circus hand. You traveled the world bringing magic and wonder to eager crowds. In that life you learned some illusion magic and sharpened your talent for performance and misdirection.","Plaintiff":"Accidents seem to follow Acquisitions Incorporated operatives. You were left barely alive by one such incident, which a magistrate blamed on the company. Before the ruling, they offered you a job and promised training in swordplay or spellcasting. You left your old life behind, signed the paperwork, and joined up for adventure.","Planar Philosopher":"You follow a philosophy that seeks to explain the planes or some hidden truth of the multiverse. Your conviction gives you strength, and you may draw on a network of like-minded thinkers, such as the factions of Sigil. You explore your ideas as you travel and spread them everywhere.","Prismari Student":"You are an artist preparing to practice your craft in the tradition of Prismari College. Your medium could be sculpture, dance, painting, music, drama, or architecture. You dream of fusing magic with creativity and bringing your grand artistic visions to life.","Purple Dragon Squire":"You pledged yourself to protect Cormyr and hope to join the Purple Dragon Knights, an elite order. First you must serve as a knight's squire, and you found a liege to teach you their ways. You must prove yourself worthy of the order's ideals of glory, honor, and strength.","Quandrix Student":"You prepared for Quandrix College by solving its math problems for applicants over many years. You are drawn to the numbers and patterns that run through the natural world. You may lean toward mathematics or physics, or toward abstract studies in metaphysics, arcana, or logic.","Rakdos Cultist":"You are a born performer who loves drama and the spotlight, and you honed your demanding skills until they look easy. You live for roaring applause and chaos in the streets. You also belong to a cult of Rakdos, a demon who loves violence and a good show, and you want to please him.","Rashemi Wanderer":"You wandered for years across the highlands of Rashemen, a harsh, windy heath dotted with ancient obelisks that imprison Fiends. Dragons, gnolls, and other deadly creatures live there. Friends are rare in such a lonely land, so you learned to keep strangers at arm's length.","Reconstructed Runaway":"You believed you had died. You awoke with your mind intact, saved as a perfect neuroprint, but in an artificial body built in the laboratories of Mordenheim Kinetics. The surgery carries a price you cannot or will not pay. To build a new life, you must escape the company that made you.","Revelry Pirate (Sailor)":"On the Menagerie Coast nearly everyone sails, but no pirates are as organized or infamous as the Revelry. You serve the Plank King and send him a share of your plunder on Darktow. If you left the crew, watch your back, since the Revelry punishes deserters until they pay up or prove too dangerous.","Rewarded":"Your life was hard until a miraculous stroke of luck changed your fate. A kind deity may have granted your deepest wish, you may have drawn a powerful card from a Deck of Many Things, or a distant relative may have left you a fortune. You left your misery behind for adventure.","Rival Intern":"You interned at a rival of Acquisitions Incorporated and came to admire its ruthless efficiency. Maybe your old employer treated you badly, or you washed out. You may hope to use what you learned there to your advantage. Either way, you now bring your talents to the company.","Ruined":"You once enjoyed luxury, love, and comfort, then lost everything. You may have been framed for crimes, had a monster wipe out your holdings, or drawn a destructive card from a Deck of Many Things. Desperation pushed you to adventuring because every other path closed. Fortune lifted you once, and it might again.","Rune Carver":"You have devoted your life to runecraft, which giants first created and many peoples now use. A master may have taught you, or you may have learned from ancient engravings. You tap the power held in runes, usually Giant runes, though many carvers also use symbols from their own languages.","Sage":"You spent years learning the lore of the multiverse. You searched through manuscripts, studied scrolls, and listened to the greatest experts on the topics that interest you. All that effort made you a master in your fields of study.","Sailor":"You sailed on a seagoing ship for years and faced fierce storms, sea monsters, and enemies who wanted your vessel sunk. The distant horizon is your first love, yet you are ready to try something new. Talk with your DM about your ship, your duties, and how you left.","Scribe":"You spent formative years in a scriptorium, a monastery that preserves knowledge, or a government office. There you learned to write with a clear hand and produce fine texts. You might write poetry, stories, or research. Above all you pay careful attention to detail, so your copies contain no mistakes.","Seer Apprentice":"You apprenticed with the Seers of Sea and Sky and learned to read small omens in the clouds above and the depths below. Their rich network of libraries furthered your education. Now you travel the world and offer advice and aid to others.","Selesnya Initiate":"You belong to a blessed community built on harmony, where nature and civilization live together by the will of the Worldsoul, Mat'Selesnya. In the Selesnya Conclave you share a longing for spiritual communion with the world. You want to spread that joy through Ravnica and resist rival guilds, by force if needed.","Shadowmasters Exile":"You trained your whole life to join the Shadowmasters, the secretive thieves' guild that quietly rules Thesk. Beyond stealth and quick reflexes, you learned the ruthlessness needed to protect its secrets. One mistake got you expelled from the order, and now you walk your own path.","Shadowmoor Expert":"You know Shadowmoor, a dim and unruly land lit only by pale moonlight, either by birth or long study. Surviving there takes sharp wits or sheer courage. Life taught you that trust is scarce, and that choosing wrongly who to rely on can cost you everything.","Shipwright":"You have spent your life around ships and the water you love. You fought in a war aboard great vessels and kept their hulls afloat with whatever you had. You also helped build a fishing boat that rescued a starving town, and you still dream of a prow you cannot carve.","Silverquill Student":"You trained for years in writing and public speaking to earn a place at Silverquill College. Your studies center on language, literature, and the force of words. You learned that speech can reveal or hide the truth, and that magic can do the same in earnest.","Simic Scientist":"You work in the Simic Combine laboratories, trying to grow a world where nature and cities fit together. You believe life must adapt, and that biomancers should help it keep pace with civilization. If your experiments also yield monstrous soldiers to defend the Combine against rival guilds, all the better.","Smuggler":"You move goods that others want hidden. You once floated a hundred swords past careless dock guards in fish barrels, and sold stolen elven wine at double its worth after a night on the river. At kinder moments, you carried refugees out of war zones or led herds away from fire.","Soldier":"War has shaped your life since youth. You learned weapons, armor, and how to stay alive on a battlefield. You may have served in a national army, a mercenary company, or a local militia that rose in a recent conflict. Work with your DM on your unit and rank.","Spellfire Initiate":"You carry spellfire, a rare gift that draws raw power from the Weave. Using it strains the body badly. To handle this sacred force safely, you have trained both your mind and your body with great discipline.","Spirit Medium":"Through odd rituals and chance meetings, you learned that you can link with the spirits of the dead and the damned. Your body lets them speak, and they sometimes grant you foresight. Their motives are not always kind, and their help leaves a lingering cost.","Urban Bounty Hunter":"You earned your living tracking people for pay, but in the city rather than the wilds. You deal with criminals and rival hunters alike and keep contacts in both circles. You might be a rooftop thief-catcher, a street-wise informant, or a hunter who mingles with nobles.","Urchin":"You grew up poor and alone on the streets, with no one to feed or protect you. You fought for scraps, slept on rooftops, and learned to watch for thieves. You survived through cunning, speed, strength, or all three, and now have enough coin to live modestly for ten days.","Uthgardt Tribe Member":"You come from an Uthgardt tribe, where cooperation and old traditions kept your people strong while other kingdoms fell. Some bands settled and traded, but the totems of Uthgar called many back to the old ways. You may be adrift from a settled tribe or seek glory for a traditional one.","Vampire Devotee":"You served a vampire or a small group of them who shared a lair. They fed on your blood many times. You may have served eagerly, hoping to become a vampire yourself, or been charmed and left with fuzzy memories. Either way, that life has ended.","Vampire Survivor":"You saw a vampire attack or lived through one. You may have fought in it or stood frozen in fear. Since then you stay alert for monsters and take pride in being prepared, so no vampire will surprise you or your friends again.","Variant City Watch (Investigator)":"You belonged to the rarer branch of a community's watch, the investigators who solve crimes after they happen. Most settlements of decent size have one or two who can read a crime scene and trail a culprit. You are skilled in Investigation rather than Athletics.","Variant Criminal (Spy)":"Your talents resemble those of a burglar or smuggler, but you honed them as an espionage agent. You may have worked as a sanctioned agent of the crown. Or you may have sold the secrets you uncovered to whoever paid the most.","Variant Entertainer (Gladiator)":"You turned combat into a show the crowd could enjoy, much like a minstrel or circus performer. You may also tumble or act. You can find work in any place that stages fights, such as an arena or a secret pit club, and may carry an unusual weapon like a trident or net.","Variant Guild Artisan (Guild Merchant)":"Rather than craft goods, you belong to a guild of traders, caravan masters, or shopkeepers. You earn your living by buying and selling what artisans make, or the materials they need. You may have hauled cargo by ship, wagon, or caravan, or run a small shop of your own.","Variant Noble (Knight)":"A knighthood is a modest noble rank, but it can lead higher. If you take this path, you gain retainers instead of a privileged position. One is a noble squire learning from you, while others might tend your horse or armor. You may carry a token from a lord or lady you admire.","Variant Sailor (Pirate)":"You spent your youth serving a ruthless pirate captain who taught you how to survive among sharks and savages. You have robbed on the high seas and sent deserving people to watery graves. Many port towns know your unsavory name, and you may take Bad Reputation instead of Ship's Passage.","Vizier":"You serve your god as a vizier, handling the tasks that help initiates on their journey. In return, the gods let you into the afterlife with the God-Pharaoh's blessing. You hope to be the finest servant possible. Most viziers are clerics, druids, or paladins.","Volstrucker Agent":"You belong to the Volstrucker, a secret order of arcane assassins serving the Cerberus Assembly. People in Dwendalian cities whisper of them as scourgers. Your duty is to silence those who threaten King Dwendal and the Assembly. The order recruits talented people already broken by tragedy.","Ward of the Sheltering Hands":"Bad luck brought you to the Sheltering Hands, a kind organization that defends the downtrodden. It helped you get back on your feet and taught you some protective magic. Its members hope you will one day shelter others in need.","Waterdhavian Noble":"You come from one of Waterdeep's great noble families, known across Faerun as eccentric, spoiled, and very rich. People expect certain things from your surname. You may be the rebel who prefers dungeons to balls, or a champion defending your family's legacy, and you might belong to a main line or a cousin branch.","Wayfarer":"You grew up on the streets among other unlucky castoffs, some friends and some rivals. You slept wherever you could and took odd jobs for food, and sometimes stole when hunger grew too sharp. Through it all you kept your pride and hope, because fate is not done with you.","Wildspacer":"You were raised in Wildspace, among asteroid miners, moon farmers, and other hardy folk. You may come from a distant settlement like the Rock of Bral, or have worked a spelljamming ship loading cargo and scraping the hull. That life has made you brave against the dangers of airless space.","Witchlight Hand":"As a youth you slipped into the Witchlight Carnival and stayed, working behind the scenes with the other hands. The carnival visits many worlds, but you see little of them. You barely know its owners, Mister Witch and Mister Light. The routine now feels dull, and bigger adventures may wait beyond its gates.","Witherbloom Student":"As a child you tried simple alchemy, hoping to enter Witherbloom College. Your reading centers on the life sciences, including biology and necromancy. Brewing odd medicinal mixtures, studying monster anatomy, and cataloging swamp plants are the studies you might pursue there.","Zhentarim Mercenary":"Maybe you needed coin, wanted a family, or just excel at getting jobs done by any means. You joined the Zhentarim, the Realms' most notorious mercenary guild. Its leaders call it a family, though it is full of dishonesty and corruption. You sharpened your cunning, reflexes, and blade to rise through its ranks."},"class":{"Artificer":"Artificers are inventors who use ingenuity and magic to give objects remarkable abilities. They treat magic as a complex system to be decoded and then put to use in spells and devices. This chapter holds all you need to play one.","Barbarian":"Barbarians are defined by rage, a wild fury that can feel like a cornered predator, a storm, or a churning sea. Some draw it from animal spirits, others from anger at a painful world. It fuels their battle frenzy, reflexes, and strength, and they distrust the soft walls of civilization.","Bard":"Bards weave magic through words and music, whether as scholars, skalds, or scoundrels. They inspire allies, shake foes, sway minds, make illusions, and heal wounds. Many believe the universe was spoken into being, and their songs capture echoes of that creation. Their great strength is versatility.","Cleric":"Clerics link the mortal world to the planes of the gods and carry divine magic. They try to embody the work of the deity they serve. Their power comes from devotion, not study. They heal and inspire allies and also curse, terrify, and burn foes.","Druid":"Druids revere nature and draw their magic from it or from a nature deity. They see themselves as extensions of its will, not its masters. Many follow the Old Faith, a mystic union with the wild. Their spells call on beasts, sun, moon, fire, and storm, and they can take animal forms.","Fighter":"Fighters are the most varied class, from questing knights and royal champions to mercenaries and bandit kings. They share unmatched skill with weapons and armor and a deep knowledge of combat. They know death well, both dealing it and facing it unflinching. Each fighter learns the basics of every fighting style.","Monk":"Monks magically harness the energy in their bodies, which most traditions call ki. They use it for striking combat skill or for subtler defense and speed. This power lets them create magical effects and surpass normal physical limits, and some of their attacks can disrupt an enemy's ki.","Mystic":"Mystics follow a strange and rare form of power. They turn inward to master their minds and explore their psyches before facing the world. They can grow huge, bolster militias with fearless resolve, or spy unseen. Most keep their abilities secret.","Paladin":"Paladins are bound by oaths to stand against evil. The oath might be sworn at an altar, in a sacred glade, or in grief before only the dead. That bond turns a devout warrior into a blessed champion. They swear to uphold justice and fight darkness wherever it hides.","Ranger":"Rangers keep a ceaseless watch in the wilds beyond distant farms. They hunt the monsters that threaten civilization's edges, such as raiders, beasts, giants, and dragons. They track prey like predators, move stealthily, and hide in brush and rubble. Many fight beside animal companions.","Rogue":"Rogues rely on skill, stealth, and enemy weaknesses to gain the upper hand. They pick locks, disarm traps, slip into shadows, and lift keys from guards. Their resourcefulness and versatility make them valuable to any party, and they work as hard on their skills as on their combat.","Expert Sidekick":"The Expert is a master of particular tasks or knowledge who favors cunning over strength. It could be a scout, musician, librarian, street kid, wily merchant, or burglar. This sidekick leans on skill and know-how.","Spellcaster Sidekick":"A sidekick who takes this path follows the ways of magic. It might be a hedge wizard, priest, soothsayer, magical performer, or someone with magic in the blood. This sidekick follows the arcane or divine path.","Warrior Sidekick":"A Warrior sidekick grows in fighting skill while it battles beside you. It could be a soldier, town guard, trained beast, or any creature honed for combat. This sidekick suits the front line.","Sorcerer":"Sorcerers have magic in their blood, from a strange bloodline, an otherworldly influence, or cosmic forces. You cannot study sorcery like a language, because the power chooses the sorcerer. The magic fills body, mind, and spirit, and its appearance is wildly unpredictable.","Warlock":"Warlocks seek hidden knowledge in the fabric of the multiverse. A pact with a mysterious being, such as a fey noble, demon, devil, hag, or Far Realm entity, grants their magic. Patrons are not gods. The bond often resembles master and apprentice, and the patron asks for occasional services.","Wizard":"Wizards are supreme spellcasters, united by the spells they cast. They draw on the weave of magic that fills the cosmos for fire, lightning, deception, and mind control. Their magic can summon monsters, glimpse the future, raise zombies, call meteors, and open portals to other worlds."},"sub":{"Path of the Ancestral Guardian":"Barbarians of this path call on the spirits of their ancestors to protect allies and punish enemies. Their rage opens a bridge to the departed, who stand guard over the living. They fight to honor family and lineage.","Path of the Storm Herald":"Barbarians of this path carry a raging storm within them. Their fury unleashes a swirling aura of nature's weather, and each rage takes on the character of the tempest they choose. They fight as living storms.","College of Glamour":"Bards of this college wield fey allure and enchantment. They enthrall crowds with beauty and charm, and their magic bends attention and affection toward them and their allies. Their illusions dazzle and delight.","College of Whispers":"Bards of this college trade in secrets and fear. They gather hidden knowledge, plant dread in the minds of enemies, and slip words into the ears of the powerful. Their whispers cut deeper than blades.","Forge Domain":"Clerics of this domain serve gods of craft and creation. They honor the hammer and the flame, making and strengthening items with divine fire. Their faith values skilled work and the sturdy things it produces.","Grave Domain":"Clerics of this domain watch over the line between life and death. They respect the dead, see that the dying find rest, and oppose undeath. Their magic guards the natural cycle of mortal lives.","Arcana Domain":"Clerics of this domain see magic and the divine as one harmony, like colors in a vast cosmic painting. They serve gods of magic and blend arcane lore with holy power.","Circle of Dreams":"Druids of this circle draw on the gentle realm of dreams and the Feywild. They soothe, heal, and protect with a restful, twilight kind of magic. Their power comes from the soft beauty of the fey.","Arcane Archer":"Fighters of this tradition infuse their arrows with magic. Elven archers long guarded the secret, training for years to shoot with supernatural skill. Each shot can carry a special magical effect to fell distant foes.","Way of the Kensei":"Monks of this tradition train with weapons as if they were extensions of their own bodies. They pursue mastery of the blade and brush with the same discipline as their martial arts. Their approach joins art and combat.","Way of the Sun Soul":"Monks of this tradition channel their ki into radiant energy. They hurl bolts of searing light and burn with inner brilliance. Their spirit shines out as a weapon against darkness.","Warrior of the Mystic Arts":"Fists can be powerful, and magic makes them more so. Monks of this path add spellcasting to their martial training and strike with arcane force alongside their unarmed blows. Their discipline joins the body and the arcane.","Oath of Conquest":"Paladins of this oath seek to crush resistance and impose order through strength. They inspire dread in enemies and command others with iron will. Their goal is victory that leaves no room for rebellion.","Oath of Redemption":"Paladins of this oath believe in peace and in giving even wrongdoers a chance to change. They try to end conflict without violence, and protect the innocent. Their path is mercy first and force only when needed.","Gloom Stalker":"Rangers of this conclave hunt in darkness and shadow. They ambush foes in dim places, strike first, and vanish. They are masters of fear and the night, and dark corners are their hunting ground.","Horizon Walker":"Rangers of this conclave guard the planar borders against otherworldly threats. They step between places in an instant and strike through the gaps between planes. They ward the world from invaders from beyond.","Mastermind":"Rogues of this archetype are schemers who rely on intrigue and insight. They read people, plan carefully, and help allies from behind the scenes. Information and influence are their greatest tools.","Swashbuckler":"Rogues of this archetype fight with flair, fast footwork, and a dash of charm. They duel with daring and style, and enjoy the spotlight. A swashbuckler turns every fight into a performance.","Divine Soul":"Sorcerers of this bloodline carry a spark of divinity. Their magic comes from a heavenly or infernal source, and they can draw on spells from the divine as well as the arcane. Their wings, when they appear, mark their nature.","Shadow Magic":"Sorcerers of this origin draw power from the Shadowfell. Darkness, fear, and the whispers of the dead answer their call. They are tied to shadow, and sometimes it seems to move on its own.","Storm Sorcery":"Sorcerers of this origin command wind, lightning, and thunder. The fury of the sky flows through them, letting them ride the gale and strike like a tempest. Their power is the weather itself.","The Hexblade":"Warlocks of this patron bind themselves to a mysterious, sentient weapon from the Shadowfell. The blade grants them martial skill and curses their enemies. Its will is dark and its bargains costly.","Vestige Patron":"The multiverse holds many gods, and not all have names in temple halls. Warlocks of this patron commune with the benevolence of a forgotten god. Theirs is a noble pursuit of an almost lost kindness.","War Magic":"Wizards of this tradition blend offense and defense, trained for the battlefield. They shield themselves while striking foes, and think tactically. Their spells serve generals and soldiers alike, and fit a mind built for war.","Conjurer":"In the Weave, physical distance means nothing. Wizards of this school learn that with enough power they can travel anywhere in existence by thought alone, and summon what they need to their side.","Enchanter":"Minds can be shaped like clay when magic is at work. Wizards of this school charm and sway others. They are warned to take care, because such power is easy to abuse.","Necromancer":"To the most powerful mages, life and death are interchangeable states. Wizards of this school study the boundary between them, wielding energies of the grave and calling on the dead to serve.","Transmuter":"Wizards of this school commune with the Weave until the limits of the body begin to fall away. They change matter and form, reshaping their surroundings and themselves, treating matter as something to be reworked."}};
